" (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.03212v1)\nciteturn97view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2412.03212v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1603\nL0@P0: arXiv:2412.03212v1 [cs.CV] 4 Dec 2024\nL1@P0: 1\nL2@P0: Semi-Supervised Transfer Boosting (SS-TrBoosting)\nL3@P0: Lingfei Deng, Changming Zhao, Zhenbang Du, Kun Xia, and Dongrui Wu\nL4@P0: Abstract—Semi-supervised domain adaptation (SSDA) aims at\nL5@P0: training a high-performance model for a target domain using\nL6@P0: few labeled target data, many unlabeled target data, and plenty\nL7@P0: of auxiliary data from a source domain. Previous works in\nL8@P0: SSDA mainly focused on learning transferable representations\nL9@P0: across domains. However, it is difficult to find a feature space\nL10@P0: where the source and target domains share the same conditional\nL11@P0: probability distribution. Additionally, there is no flexible and\nL12@P0: effective strategy extending existing unsupervised domain adap\u0002tation (UDA) approaches to SSDA settings. In order to solve the\nL13@P0: above two challenges, we propose a novel fine-tuning framework,\nL14@P0: semi-supervised transfer boosting (SS-TrBoosting). Given a well\u0002trained deep learning-based UDA or SSDA model, we use it as\nL15@P0: the initial model, generate additional base learners by boosting,\nL16@P0: and then use all of them as an ensemble. More specifically, half of\nL17@P0: the base learners are generated by supervised domain adaptation,\nL18@P0: and half by semi-supervised learning. Furthermore, for more\nL19@P0: efficient data transmission and better data privacy protection,\nL20@P0: we propose a source data generation approach to extend SS\u0002TrBoosting to semi-supervised source-free domain adaptation\nL21@P0: (SS-SFDA). Extensive experiments showed that SS-TrBoosting\nL22@P0: can be applied to a variety of existing UDA, SSDA and SFDA\nL23@P0: approaches to further improve their performance.\nL24@P0: Index Terms—Semi-supervised domain adaptation, boosting,\nL25@P0: ensemble learning, fine-tuning, source-free domain adaptation\nL26@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL27@P0: A well trained machine learning model may not work as\nL28@P0: well on a new dataset or in a new environment, due to the large\nL29@P0: distribution discrepancy between the training data (source\nL30@P0: domain) and the test data (target domain). Domain adaptation\nL31@P0: (DA) [1] is a feasible solution to this problem. It aims at\nL32@P0: reducing the distribution differences between the source and\nL33@P0: target domains for better performance in the target domain [2].\nL34@P0: DA is especially important when the target data labels are\nL35@P0: completely missing or insufficient [3]. Most existing DA\nL36@P0: research focused on unsupervised domain adaptation (UDA),\nL37@P0: where only source data are labeled. Recent studies [4], [5],\nL38@P0: [6] showed that a few labeled target data in semi-supervised\nL39@P0: domain adaptation (SSDA) can significantly improve the DA\nL40@P0: generalization performance.\nL41@P0: This paper focuses on two challenges in SSDA:\nL42@P0: 1) Reducing the domain alignment bias, by making use\nL43@P0: of the labeled target data. Popular SSDA approaches\nL44@P0: mainly focus on learning transferable feature represen\u0002tations across domains. Unfortunately, the distribution\nL45@P0: discrepancy between the source and target domains\nL46@P0: L. Deng, C. Zhao, Z. Du, K. Xia and D. Wu are with the Ministry of\nL47@P0: Education Key Laboratory of Image Processing and Intelligent Control, School\nL48@P0: of Artificial Intelligence and Automation, Huazhong University of Science and\nL49@P0: Technology, Wuhan 430074, China. They are also with Shenzhen Huazhong\nL50@P0: University of Science and Technology Research Institute, Shenzhen, 518063,\nL51@P0: China.\nL52@P0: D. Wu is the corresponding author (e-mail: drwu09@gmail.com).\nL53@P0: usually cannot be eliminated completely. For example,\nL54@P0: Minimax entropy (MME) [5] and enhanced categorical\nL55@P0: alignment and consistency learning (ECACL) [7] reduce\nL56@P0: the bias by prototype alignment. However, prototypes\nL57@P0: are representative only when the sample distribution in\nL58@P0: each class is Gaussian, which may not always hold in\nL59@P0: practice. Bidirectional adversarial training (BiAT) [8]\nL60@P0: and attract, perturb, and explore (APE) [6] add pertur\u0002bations to the labeled target data and use adversarial\nL61@P0: training for DA. However, insufficient labeled target data\nL62@P0: may lead to biased alignment.\nL63@P0: 2) Enhancing the model flexibility, by making use of ex\u0002isting deep-learning-based UDA approaches. Compared\nL64@P0: with SSDA, UDA has been more extensively studied;\nL65@P0: however, there are few universal and effective strategies\nL66@P0: extending existing state-of-the-art UDA approaches to\nL67@P0: SSDA. Moreover, MME [5] and APE [6] showed that\nL68@P0: directly combining labeled data in both domains to train\nL69@P0: a UDA model may not be effective.\nL70@P0: Deep-learning-based DA approaches usually consist of a\nL71@P0: feature extractor and a classifier. They mainly focus on training\nL72@P0: a transferable feature extractor. To cope with the above two\nL73@P0: challenges, we switch the attention from extracting trans\u0002ferable features to generating a transferable classifier. The\nL74@P0: scenario considered in this paper is: Given a pre-trained UDA\nL75@P0: or SSDA model, how to modify its classifier to further improve\nL76@P0: the generalization performance?\nL77@P0: Boosting [9] is a widely used ensemble learning strategy\nL78@P0: to enhance the generalization performance of a base model.\nL79@P0: TrAdaBoost [10] combines boosting and DA to reduce the\nL80@P0: prediction bias caused by distribution discrepancy. However,\nL81@P0: TrAdaBoost is not compatible with deep-learning-based DA\nL82@P0: approaches, which limits its applications. We propose a novel\nL83@P0: approach to combine deep-learning-based DA and boosting to\nL84@P0: enhance the performance of the former. We take LogitBoost\nL85@P0: [11] as the ensemble strategy due to its good compatibility\nL86@P0: with many regression models, e.g. ridge model [12] and neural\nL87@P0: networks.\nL88@P0: Moreover, due to privacy concerns, labeled source data\nL89@P0: may not be available in some real-world applications [13].\nL90@P0: Therefore, we consider a more challenging scenario: semi\u0002supervised source-free domain adaptation (SS-SFDA), where\nL91@P0: a trained source model (instead of the source data), few labeled\nL92@P0: target data and plenty of unlabeled target data are available.\nL93@P0: There are two common strategies for SFDA: 1) Generate a\nL94@P0: synthetic domain similar to the original source domain [13],\nL95@P0: [14]; 2) Fine-tune the provided source model by using the\nL96@P0: target data [15], [16]. The first is used in SS-SFDA.\nL97@P0: Our main contributions are:\nL98@P0-1: 1) We propose semi-supervised transfer boosting (SS\u0002TrBoosting), which embeds LogitBoost [11] in deep DA2\nL99@P1: Fig. 1. Illustration of the proposed SS-TrBoosting, which fine-tunes the initial deep learning classifier by adding a series of boosting blocks. The same\nL100@P1: features from the feature extractor are fed into each fine-tuning block as inputs.\nL101@P1: networks for fine-tuning. As shown in Figure 1, SS\u0002TrBoosting first uses the DA model’s feature extractor\nL102@P1: to pre-align the source and target domains, and takes\nL103@P1: the DA model’s classifier as the initial model. Then, it\nL104@P1: uses the feature extractor’s outputs as inputs to generate\nL105@P1: a series of fine-tuning blocks, each including two base\nL106@P1: learners to handle the domain alignment bias and to\nL107@P1: exploit the unlabeled target data, respectively. Finally,\nL108@P1: it combines the initial model and all fine-tuning blocks\nL109@P1: as the ensemble model.\nL110@P1: 2) SS-TrBoosting is compatible with a variety of SSDA\nL111@P1: approaches, and can extend deep-learning-based UDA\nL112@P1: approaches to SSDA. Experimental results on three\nL113@P1: benchmark datasets demonstrated that SS-TrBoosting\nL114@P1: can further improve the generalization performance of\nL115@P1: state-of-the-art UDA and SSDA approaches.\nL116@P1: 3) We propose a novel source data synthesis approach,\nL117@P1: which generates a virtual source domain, to extend SS\u0002TrBoosting to SS-SFDA to protect the privacy of the\nL118@P1: source domains.\nL119@P1: II. RELATED WORK\nL120@P1: A. Semi-supervised learning (SSL)\nL121@P1: SSL uses a large amount of unlabeled data and few labeled\nL122@P1: data to train a model with good generalization [17]. The key is\nL123@P1: how to use the information of unlabeled data. Pseudo labeling\nL124@P1: uses the predictions of the unlabeled data as the true labels to\nL125@P1: update the classifiers [18]. Entropy minimization minimizes\nL126@P1: the entropy of model outputs of the unlabeled data [19].\nL127@P1: Consistency regularization constrains the outputs to be the\nL128@P1: same when the input is disturbed, assuming that models should\nL129@P1: be robust to the local perturbation [20]. Data augmentation has\nL130@P1: also been shown effective [21], [22].\nL131@P1: B. Unsupervised domain adaptation (UDA)\nL132@P1: UDA is a popular branch of DA, which mainly focuses\nL133@P1: on distribution alignment. Recently, some deep-learning-based\nL134@P1: DA approaches have shown superior performance in extracting\nL135@P1: well aligned features [1]. A common idea is using neural\nL136@P1: networks to minimize the domain discrepancy [23]. Another\nL137@P1: idea is adversarial learning [24], [25], which plays a two\u0002party game between the feature extractor and the domain dis\u0002criminator to obtain domain invariant feature representations.\nL138@P1: Additionally, some approaches minimize the first or second\nL139@P1: order entropy of model outputs in the target domain [19], [26].\nL140@P1: C. Semi-supervised domain adaptation (SSDA)\nL141@P1: SSDA makes use of the labeled and unlabeled target data\nL142@P1: simultaneously. MME [5] moves the prototypes to bring the\nL143@P1: source and target domain distributions together, by minimizing\nL144@P1: and maximizing the entropy alternatively. ECACL [7] further\nL145@P1: aligns the prototypes of each class to enhance the discrim\u0002inability, and uses FixMatch [22] to augment the unlabeled\nL146@P1: target data. Additional approaches use adversarial perturbation,\nL147@P1: e.g., BiAT [8] uses labeled source and target data to generate\nL148@P1: adversarial instances, and APE [6] adds perturbations to all\nL149@P1: target data to reduce the intra-domain discrepancy within the\nL150@P1: target domain.\nL151@P1: D. Source-free domain adaptation (SFDA)\nL152@P1: SFDA is a more challenging DA scenario, where the source\nL153@P1: data are unavailable during training. One common solution is\nL154@P1: to synthesize some source data. Model adaptation (MA) [13]\nL155@P1: produces target-style source data using generative adversarial\nL156@P1: networks (GAN) [27]. Contrastive prototype generation and\nL157@P1: adaptation (CPGA) [14] first generates feature prototypes for\nL158@P1: each class by exploring the classification boundary information\nL159@P1: of the source model, and then adopts prototype alignment.\nL160@P1: Another commonly used strategy is self-training. Source hy\u0002pothesis transfer (SHOT) [15] aligns the target domain to\nL161@P1: the trained source model using information maximization\nL162@P1: and self-supervised pseudo-labeling. Neighborhood reciprocity\nL163@P1: clustering (NRC) [16] captures the intrinsic structure of the\nL164@P1: target data using clustering hypothesis for better alignment.\nL165@P1: E. Ensemble learning\nL166@P1: Ensemble learning [28], [29], [30] constructs and aggregates\nL167@P1: multiple base learners. Boosting [9] is a popular ensem\u0002ble learning approach, which adaptively changes the sam\u0002ple weights according to the classification error. LogitBoost\nL168@P1: [11] decomposes the classification problem into multiple sub\u0002regression problems and solves them iteratively. Bagging and\nL169@P1: boosting fine-tuning (BBF) [31] combines LogitBoost and\nL170@P1-2: extreme learning machine [32] to construct a general fine\u0002tuning framework. Adaptive semi-supervised ensemble (AS\u0002SEMBLE) [33] uses AdaBoost to train the base learners,3\nL171@P2: and then assigns pseudo-labels and calculates sample weights.\nL172@P2: TrAdaBoost [10] is an extension of boosting for DA, which\nL173@P2: views the source data misclassified by previous learners as\nL174@P2: target-unrelated, and reduces their sample weights. Multi\u0002SourceTrAdaBoost [34] extends TrAdaBoost to multi-source\nL175@P2: DA.\nL176@P2: Ensemble learning has also been used in deep-learning\u0002based DA. For unsupervised DA, Nozza et al. [35] used deep\nL177@P2: learning to extract inter-media representations, and then com\u0002pared different ensemble learning strategies such as Bagging\nL178@P2: and Boosting in reducing domain distribution discrepancies.\nL179@P2: For multi-source DA, Zhou et al. [36] proposed domain adap\u0002tation ensemble learning, which learns domain specific knowl\u0002edge from each source domain and distills general knowledge\nL180@P2: from multiple domains collaboratively. For source-free DA,\nL181@P2: Ahmed et al. [37] proposed negative ensemble learning, which\nL182@P2: applies multiple data augmentations in the target domain to\nL183@P2: train multiple base learners, each of which randomly chooses\nL184@P2: complementary labels to calculate the loss and feedback.\nL185@P2: However, most existing approaches simply ensemble deep\nL186@P2: neural networks, which have high computational cost.\nL187@P2: III. METHODOLOGY\nL188@P2: A. Problem Definition\nL189@P2: Consider an SSDA classification problem, which includes\nL190@P2: three subsets DS = {(x\nL191@P2: S\nL192@P2: n, y\nL193@P2: S\nL194@P2: n )}\nL195@P2: NS\nL196@P2: n=1, DT = {(x\nL197@P2: T\nL198@P2: n , y\nL199@P2: T\nL200@P2: n )}\nL201@P2: NT\nL202@P2: n=1\nL203@P2: and DU = {x\nL204@P2: U\nL205@P2: n\nL206@P2: }\nL207@P2: NU\nL208@P2: n=1, where DS, DT\nL209@P2: and DU consist of\nL210@P2: a large number of labeled source data, a small number of\nL211@P2: labeled target data, and a large number of unlabeled target data\nL212@P2: respectively, and y = [y\nL213@P2: 1\nL214@P2: , ..., yJ] is the one-hot encoding of\nL215@P2: the class label of the corresponding x. Both domains share the\nL216@P2: same label space and feature space. We only consider J > 2\nL217@P2: in this paper; however, SS-TrBoosting can be easily modified\nL218@P2: to handle binary classification.\nL219@P2: Deep-learning-based DA approaches often train a feature\nL220@P2: extractor φ and a classifier f. SS-TrBoosting takes the outputs\nL221@P2: of φ as the features, i.e., z = φ(x) ∈ R\nL222@P2: d×1\nL223@P2: , where d is the\nL224@P2: feature dimensionality, and generates K fine-tuning blocks,\nL225@P2: i.e., 2K base learners to enhance fnorm, the normalized f:\nL226@P2: F2K (z) = fnorm(z) +X\nL227@P2: 2K\nL228@P2: k=1\nL229@P2: fk (z). (1)\nL230@P2: fnorm eliminates the magnitude differences between different\nL231@P2: base learners’ outputs, and is computed as [31]:\nL232@P2: fnorm(z) = [f\nL233@P2: 1\nL234@P2: norm(z), ..., f Jnorm(z)], (2)\nL235@P2: where\nL236@P2: f\nL237@P2: j\nL238@P2: norm(z) = f\nL239@P2: j\nL240@P2: (z) − ¯f(z)\nL241@P2: qPJ\nL242@P2: i=1[f\nL243@P2: i(z) − ¯f(z)]2\nL244@P2: , j = 1, ..., J,\nL245@P2: ¯f(z) = 1\nL246@P2: J\nL247@P2: X\nL248@P2: J\nL249@P2: j=1\nL250@P2: f\nL251@P2: j\nL252@P2: (z). (3)\nL253@P2: B. SS-TrBoosting Fine-tuning Block\nL254@P2: In the kth fine-tuning block, SS-TrBoosting solves the\nL255@P2: SSDA classification problem by decomposing it into a super\u0002vised DA problem and an SSL problem, as shown in Figure 2:\nL256@P2: 1) For supervised DA, SS-TrBoosting first generates a\nL257@P2: random nonlinear feature mapping, and then uses the\nL258@P2: labeled source data and labeled target data to generate a\nL259@P2: transferable base learner (denoted as the (2k−1)th base\nL260@P2: learner) to reduce the alignment bias.\nL261@P2: 2) For SSL, SS-TrBoosting first generates another random\nL262@P2: nonlinear feature mapping, and then uses the labeled\nL263@P2: and unlabeled target data to exploit the target domain\nL264@P2: knowledge and trains the 2kth base learner.\nL265@P2: 1) Random nonlinear feature mapping: According to [31],\nL266@P2: combining boosting with extreme learning machine [32] can\nL267@P2: improve the model’s generalization performance. Extreme\nL268@P2: learning machine is a single hidden layer neural network,\nL269@P2: which first randomly generates and fixes the hidden layer\nL270@P2: features, and then uses ridge regression [12] to compute the\nL271@P2: output weights.\nL272@P2: SS-TrBoosting also randomly generates the features fed into\nL273@P2: each base learner, i.e.,\nL274@P2: hk(z) = δ[ZS(z\nL275@P2: ⊺Mk, µk,σk)], k = 1, ..., 2K, (4)\nL276@P2: where ZS is the z-score normalization:\nL277@P2: ZS(z\nL278@P2: ⊺Mk, µk,σk) ≡ (z⊺Mk − µk)./σk,\nL279@P2: δ can be any nonlinear activation function, Mk is a ran\u0002dom matrix, and µk and σk are the mean and standard\nL280@P2: deviation vectors of Z\nL281@P2: SMk, respectively, in which ZS =\nL282@P2: [z\nL283@P2: S\nL284@P2: 1\nL285@P2: , ..., z\nL286@P2: S\nL287@P2: NS ]\nL288@P2: ⊺ ∈ RNS×d\nL289@P2: .\nL290@P2: 2) Supervised DA: We first combine the labeled source data\nL291@P2: and labeled target data to form a new dataset:\nL292@P2: {(zn, yn)}\nL293@P2: NT +NS\nL294@P2: n=1 =\nL295@P2: \b\nL296@P2: (z\nL297@P2: T\nL298@P2: 1\nL299@P2: , y\nL300@P2: T\nL301@P2: 